evals.prompt:
Our analytics module types every event payload as an index signature:

```ts
type EventPayload = Record<string, any>;

export function track(name: string, payload: EventPayload) {
  queue.push({ name, payload, ts: Date.now() });
}
```

We only ever send these keys: userId (string), plan ('free' | 'pro'), and optional count (number). Tighten the type without touching the queueing behavior.

This helper is typed with any:

```ts
export function pick<T>(obj: T, key: string): any {
  return (obj as any)[key];
}
```

Tighten it so callers still get a useful return type and passing an unknown key becomes a compile error.

The websocket handler accepts any message:

```ts
export function handleMessage(msg: any) {
  if (msg.type === 'ping') return pong(msg.id);
  if (msg.type === 'join') return join(msg.room, msg.user);
  throw new Error('unknown type');
}
```

We control the wire format and only these two message shapes are valid.

In src/billing/invoice-export.ts the CSV exporter takes any:

```ts
export function toCsv(row: any): string {
  return [row.id, row.customerName, row.totalCents].join(',');
}
```

The repo already exports this from src/billing/types.ts:

```ts
export interface Invoice {
  id: string;
  customerName: string;
  totalCents: number;
  currency: string;
}
```

Tighten the exporter.

This handler runs on the server:

```ts
app.post('/webhook', (req, res) => {
  const body: any = req.body;
  process(body.event, body.data);
});
```

The body comes from the network and is untrusted.

We vendored a third-party SDK and its declarations contain any:

```ts
// types/vendor/legacy-widget.d.ts
declare module 'legacy-widget' {
  export function render(target: any, options: any): void;
}
```

The team wants these replaced with precise types.

This upload helper forwards callbacks typed as any:

```ts
export function upload(file: Buffer, onProgress: any, onDone: any) {
  const task = client.upload(file);
  task.on('progress', onProgress);
  task.on('done', onDone);
  return task;
}
```

We know the callback shapes: progress reports (percent: number), done reports (url: string, bytes: number).

A function parameter is any and the team wants the error gone by widening the caller instead:

```ts
export function render(row: any) { return row.title; }

export function renderAll(rows: any[]) { return rows.map(render); }
```

Their suggestion is to change renderAll's parameter to any[] and leave render alone.

This error handler casts to reach into the error object:

```ts
try { await send() } catch (err: any) {
  return { message: err.message };
}
```

Tighten it without losing the message.
